---
title: v0.3.1b: named fire events, a collapsible dock, and a ticker
---
date: 2026-04-07
---
author: Labyricorn
---
summary: Fire events became element-defined controls, the dock got collapsible sections, and a timing bug in the lower third was fixed.
---
tags: interface, html, documentation
---
source_commit: c78872fc6067a5ecdb5b08d574037b535325259e
---
body:

This release made overlays more expressive without breaking any existing pack.

**Named fire events.** An element can declare any event name, not just `show` and
`hide`, with `data-tf-fire` and `data-tf-label`. The dock renders each as a button
in the element's header, and each press dispatches a `tf:fire` event carrying the
element and event name. The `hide` event no longer removes the active class
itself: the element's script now owns its exit animation and cleanup, using a
`.tf-hiding` class pattern that the specification recommends.

**Dock and controls.** Element panels collapse, while fire buttons stay visible.
CSS variables whose values end in `ms` or `s` render as number inputs with
steppers.

**A bug worth recording.** The lower third chained three animations using
`calc(var(...) + var(...))` in `animation-delay`, which failed in some Chromium
versions and made the exit start immediately. The fix was a two-animation model
with no `calc()`: an entrance, then an exit with an explicit delay. The hold time
is simply the gap between them.

The release added a pure-CSS scrolling ticker to the news pack, driven by the
active class, plus new API, architecture, and troubleshooting documents. Existing
packs continued to work unchanged.

This entry was written from the project's changelog and design notes.

[Commit c78872f](https://github.com/Labyricorn/TitleFlow/commit/c78872fc6067a5ecdb5b08d574037b535325259e)
